1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,400
Why does a soap bubble wear a rainbow

2
00:00:06,400 --> 00:00:08,800
and why does it keep changing?

3
00:00:08,800 --> 00:00:13,200
Its skin is a film of soapy water,

4
00:00:13,200 --> 00:00:17,200
only a tiny fraction of a millimetre thick.

5
00:00:17,200 --> 00:00:19,200
When white light hits it,

6
00:00:19,200 --> 00:00:22,400
some reflects from the outer surface.

7
00:00:22,400 --> 00:00:24,800
The rest enters the film,

8
00:00:24,800 --> 00:00:27,200
bounces off the inner surface,

9
00:00:27,200 --> 00:00:31,200
and emerges beside the first reflection.

10
00:00:31,200 --> 00:00:33,600
Those two light waves overlap.

11
00:00:33,600 --> 00:00:36,400
Depending on the film's thickness

12
00:00:36,400 --> 00:00:38,000
and the viewing angle,

13
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:41,200
some wavelengths reinforce each other

14
00:00:41,200 --> 00:00:43,200
while others cancel.

15
00:00:43,200 --> 00:00:47,200
That is thin-film interference.

16
00:00:47,200 --> 00:00:54,000
Gravity slowly drains liquid down the bubble,

17
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:58,000
changing the film's thickness,

18
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:01,200
so the colours shift across its surface.

19
00:01:01,200 --> 00:01:04,000
Then comes the eerie final clue:

20
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:08,000
where the film becomes extremely thin,

21
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:13,200
reflected light can cancel across the visible spectrum,

22
00:01:13,200 --> 00:01:14,800
leaving a dark patch.

23
00:01:14,800 --> 00:01:17,600
The rainbow is made by light waves

24
00:01:17,600 --> 00:01:19,200
adding—and subtracting.